A||N||5||oneof547||Tail light buffing????||||1||
Z||000000||oneof547||02-07-2001||12:02 AM||||Just a quick question regarding the plexiglass like tailights...Mine have fine scratches in them and a sum hazy spots on the outside.. they have been thier since i bought the car. I am now in the process of repainting etc... and would like to know if anyone has used any kind of waxes or polishes for this area.. if so what brand and what method did u use??...THANKS<BR>oneof547<BR>88formula t-tops<P>------------------<BR>oneof547||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||02-07-2001||12:09 AM||||I use "Eagle One" brand plastic polish & protectant (part# 4010612).<P>It seems to work pretty good......give it a try.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||02-07-2001||12:51 AM||||Get a micro plastci polish kit from a hobby store ..Like new!<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||02-07-2001||08:08 AM||||WElcome to the forum!<P>What color is your Formula? I saw a white one rolling down Southern BLVD a month or so ago when I was down to visit my father in Wellington FL.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">    <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||02-07-2001||10:18 AM||||I had the same thing with my notchback taillights.  I wetsanded them with 400/600/800/1500, and then buffed them with a plastic only buffing compound on a slow speed buffer.  You do not want to go too fast, or you will burn and melt the plastic,  I have done this.  After buffing them, I followed up with Mequiar's #10 plastic polish.  They look  great.  I will post pics if I can find them.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||PJ||02-07-2001||10:32 PM||||Plastic polish is one way to go. Comes in two or three different grit compounds. Get it where they sell polycarbonate or lexan sheet stock.<BR>Another great product is called Micromesh. It is a series of 10 or so progressively finer grit sandpaper sheets. The final grit size is so fine you won't need to polish. This is the stuff they use on aircraft windows. And crankshaft journals.||||reg||1
